The issue here is about **when** you can call `super()`. In Java, the `super()` call must be the **first statement** in a constructor - it cannot come after any other statements like `System.out.println("Before super call")`.

Think of it this way: when you create a `Student` object, Java needs to first ensure that the parent class (`Person`) is properly initialized before initializing the child class. So Java expects the `super()` call to happen immediately when the constructor starts executing.

What happens in your code is that Java sees the `System.out.println("Before super call")` before the `super()` call, which violates this rule. This is why you're getting a compilation error.

The correct approach would be to put the `super()` call at the beginning of the constructor, before any other statements.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
class Person {
    Person() { System.out.println("Person constructor"); }
}
class Student extends Person {
    String name;
    Student(String name) {
        super();
        this.name = name;
        System.out.println("Student constructor");
    }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student student = new Student(new Scanner(System.in).nextLine());
        System.out.println("Student Name: " + student.name);
    }
}
```

